00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.170
Anyway, so let's think
about software development.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.170
So when you think of
software development, what

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.110
does come to your mind first?

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:50.770
Anyone?

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:51.540
Anyone?

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.267
AUDIENCE: DevOps.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:53.100
METE ATAMEL: DevOps.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.720
OK, what else?

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.510
Software development, is it fun?

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.850
Yes, maybe, not so much.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.600
Is it easy?

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.530
Maybe.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.530
Well for me, when I think of
software development nowadays,

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:07.400
I think of this.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.780
I think software
development is really hard.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.380
The only people who really
told me that it's not hard

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.167
is usually the product managers.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.750
They think that
everything is possible

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.390
and you should do-- you should
have done everything yesterday.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.280
But if you talk to software
engineers, it's really hard.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.330
And the thing is, it's
not getting any easier.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.246
You would think that
with the passage of time,

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.640
with the tools and
cloud and everything,

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.290
things would get easier.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:34.750
But that's not the case,
at least in my experience.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.280
So anyone remembers these?

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.170
Yeah, some people?

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:38.840
OK.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.300
It's always good to have a
few people who remember these,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.550
so I'm not the oldest
guy in the room.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.720
Basically back in the--

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.160
I didn't use the bigger ones.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.520
But I used the small one,
the one in the middle.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.040
Back in the day, you
would have your MS-DOS.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.530
You would put in
your floppy drive.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.340
You would close it.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.950
You would turn your computer on.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.820
It will load the operating
system in like a minute or two,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.300
and then you would get the a
prompt, and then from then on,

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.160
you would take the
operating system out.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.960
You put the Basic--

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.990
I think I used QBasic
or GWBasic when I first

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.220
started programming.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.760
And then you would just get into
Basic and start coding, right?

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.310
And the thing is
there was no internet.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.870
There was nothing.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.950
So this was literally the only
thing I had to worry about.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.020
When you think of
microservices, there's

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.120
this notion of bounded context.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.730
Well, back then, the
context was this disk.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.277
And it was like physical.

00:03:30.277 --> 00:03:31.110
So you could see it.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.120
You could feel it.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.000
And life was really easy.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.950
I remember all I
had was this disk.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.110
And then I had a book on Basic.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.330
And I would just look at
my book, do some stuff.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.302
Look at my book, do some stuff.

00:03:43.302 --> 00:03:45.260
I mean at some point it
was hard, because there

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.310
was no Stack Overflow.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:50.030
So if you are stuck, there's
no one to ask help for.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.370
But I like the simplicity
of those times.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.070
Because all I had to worry
about was just I have a problem

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.410
and I want to
solve that problem.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.540
Since then, a lot
of things changed.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.400
The first thing is
obviously internet.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.450
And then after the internet,
we had all these things

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:05.210
that happened.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.270
So we had object-oriented
programming, virtual machines,

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.680
cloud computing, caching,
machine learning.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.660
So it's really impossible to
stay on top of all of this.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.610
So what I try to do as
a software engineer,

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.680
is I pick and
choose what I like,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.642
and try to learn
as much as I can.

00:04:20.642 --> 00:04:23.100
For example, microservices is
one thing that I really like.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.990
So I try to learn
as much as I can.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.450
And I'm trying to learn
about machine learning.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.100
Because that's where
the programming is

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.510
going to evolve into.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.100
But then I also try
to get an overview

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.000
of the rest of the
landscape, so I know what

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.220
the landscape is all about.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.900
But it is impossible to learn
and know everything nowadays.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.390
So nowadays if you have
a problem, what you do

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.890
is you start with
the code again.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:46.884
So you write your code.

00:04:46.884 --> 00:04:48.800
So let's assume that
this is the piece of code

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.795
that you wrote to
solve a problem.

00:04:50.795 --> 00:04:53.000
It's not on the disk anymore.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.430
Its usually hosted on
some kind of server.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.830
So you find a server
somewhere, and host your code.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.580
It can be your server
maybe, or maybe

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.135
its some Cloud provider server.

00:05:01.135 --> 00:05:02.010
So it doesn't matter.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.700
Your code runs on a
server somewhere, right?

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.590
And then someone
says, what happens

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.000
if that server goes down.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.500
What do you do?

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.610
So you have to have redundancy.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.880
So what you do is you basically
add a number of servers.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.810
It depends on how many you
want, but usually 2-3 is enough.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:21.030
And then you run the same
code on those machines.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.670
But then the clients,
they need to know

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:24.290
the IPs of those machines.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.400
That's not ideal.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.830
So what you do is you
create a load balancer that

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.650
has a stable virtual
IP, and then the clients

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:31.970
will talk to the load balancer.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.640
And it will get to the
machines, as it needs to.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.280
So all of a sudden, you have
your code, your machines,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.660
and your load balancer.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.720
But then how do you know your
machines are actually healthy?

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:43.850
So to do that,
what you usually do

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:46.160
is you create what's
called a health endpoint.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.710
And that will check what it
means for your application

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.200
to be healthy.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.950
And then if it's
healthy, it will say, OK.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.080
If it's not healthy,
it will say not OK.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.600
But someone has to actually
check these health endpoints.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.910
So what you have is
usually a health checker

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:01.850
that will ping these endpoints
and check whether that machine

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:02.949
is healthy or not.

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:04.490
And if it's not
healthy, you probably

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.010
want to create a new machine
and add your code there as well.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.290
So this is great for one zone,
everything lives in one zone.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:14.370
But you are relying
on a single zone.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.640
And as we know, even in the
cloud, sometimes zones go down.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:18.530
So what do you do?

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:20.840
You create the whole
setup in a different zone

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.300
with the same load balancers,
same machines, same health

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.644
check and everything.

00:06:24.644 --> 00:06:25.310
So this is good.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.750
So I have redundancy.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.470
But then how do I
keep the two in sync?

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.690
So that's another problem.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.610
So what you usually do is you
have something in the middle.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.260
You either have a
master or a slave,

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.680
or you have some
kind of task here

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.430
in the middle, where
the state from one zone

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.320
gets replicated to
the other states.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.750
So that way they are in sync.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:45.470
So that's great.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.060
I have two zones
that are replicated

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:47.880
and everything is good.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.088
But what if my application
is getting really popular,

00:06:50.088 --> 00:06:51.260
and I need to scale up?

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.510
Right You can scale up
manually as you need to.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:59.270
But it's difficult. Because
usually applications,

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.230
they have cyclic graphs.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.200
So people use them a
lot during the day.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:04.936
But at night they
don't use them as much.

00:07:04.936 --> 00:07:06.560
So you usually scale
up during the day,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.360
and you scale down
during the night.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.860
So no one wants to
do that manually.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.520
So what you usually do is
you create auto scaler that

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.860
will look at your load
somehow, and figure out

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.110
how to scale up and down.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.090
So this is how it
looks like nowadays.

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.200
And it gets much more
complicated than this.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.050
But this is like
the basic scenario

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:26.850
that you implement usually.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:31.030
And this is for
running a piece of code

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:33.610
on a single type of machine.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.420
If you need to run different
types of applications

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.650
in different languages, on
different kinds of machines,

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.670
then you have more work to do.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:45.290
If you want to deploy a new
version of your application,

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.800
then you need to worry
about this whole machines

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.590
and zones and stuff like that.

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:53.420
So it's not so easy to deploy
your new version anymore.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.610
If there's something wrong
with that new version

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.693
and you need to roll back
to the previous version,

00:07:57.693 --> 00:08:00.920
you need to do that in a
consistent and stable way.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.860
If you want to do
configuration and secrets

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.320
of your application, you
need to manage them somehow.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.528
You need to make sure that
they are centrally managed

00:08:08.528 --> 00:08:11.090
and you need to somehow
get them working with all

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.700
the machines in a certain way.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.305
And if you need to do
scripts, because usually

00:08:16.305 --> 00:08:17.930
when the machines
come up, they usually

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.550
have some kind of
scripts to run.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.046
So that's not the code.

00:08:21.046 --> 00:08:23.420
But it's actually things that
make your application work.

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:24.840
So you need to manage
those scripts as well.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.464
So there's all sorts
of things that you

00:08:26.464 --> 00:08:28.070
need to worry about right now.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:30.890
So if you're like me, you're
probably feeling like this guy

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:31.790
right now.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:35.614
You just want to write some
code to solve some problem.

00:08:35.614 --> 00:08:37.280
But now all of a
sudden you have to deal

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:38.280
with all these problems.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.720
So the reality of today
is kind of like this.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.280
You want to care about the
code that you're writing.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:44.900
That's what you're trying to do.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:47.940
But then in reality this is
all you are trying to do.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.480
You're are trying to create
all this infrastructure

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.330
so that your code runs
in a consistent, stable,

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:58.250
and resilient way, basically.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:01.340
And actually in one
place that I worked

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:05.950
before, this ratio of
coding versus operations

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:09.920
got so bad for me that I
was spending 90% of my time

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.100
trying to manage all of this,
rather than writing the code.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:16.080
So this balance
has to be really,

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.600
really maintained properly.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.600
So what do we do?

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.210
Well, in the good old
days up until recently,

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.660
what we used to do is that
first we write our code.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.600
And then we pass that
code to QA for testing.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.810
Because as software engineers,
we don't test our code.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.250
That's someone else's job.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.390
So we'll pass it to
QA and say, just go

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.360
ahead and test this code.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:40.640
They will find some bugs.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:41.460
Send them to us.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.040
We will fight back and forth
whether they are bugs or not.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.820
Eventually we will fix the bugs
and if the QA person is happy,

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.070
then we'll pass it
to operations people

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.954
and let them deal
with it in production.

00:09:51.954 --> 00:09:52.620
Let them run it.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.030
Because as software
engineers, we

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.238
don't have to worry about
running code in production.

00:09:56.238 --> 00:09:59.190
We can just let someone
else worry about that.

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:01.320
So we took this hands-off
approach basically

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:03.870
where we just write
our code, and just

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:07.110
give it to people for testing,
and give it to people to run,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.450
and it's not really our problem.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.660
Well, this didn't really work,
so that's why we have DevOps.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.760
So I'm sure most of you know
what DevOps is, but in DevOps,

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.120
basically the idea is
that you write your code,

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.417
you test your code,
you run your code.

00:10:21.417 --> 00:10:23.250
And if there's something
broken, you fix it.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:26.780
And you basically have the
full lifecycle of your code.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.190
There are good things and
bad things about DevOps.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.067
The good thing is that
you have that feedback

00:10:31.067 --> 00:10:33.150
loop that you need as a
software engineer to write

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:34.410
really good code.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.264
Because you have an
incentive not to wake up

00:10:36.264 --> 00:10:37.680
in the middle of
the night, so you

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.770
make sure that your code is
resilient and actually works,

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:41.640
I've seen it work.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.130
But then with DevOps,
you need it to make sure

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.140
that you have the
tools and the resources

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:49.230
and the structure that
you need to do it right.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.047
So I've seen some places
where they just think

00:10:52.047 --> 00:10:53.130
that they're doing DevOps.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.550
But what they're
doing is actually

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.550
they're just deploying
code as fast as possible,

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.460
and let the code
burn in production.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.089
And then with that, the whole
development team burns as well.

00:11:02.089 --> 00:11:03.630
Because they're
spending so much time

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.046
trying to fix
things in production

00:11:05.046 --> 00:11:06.870
rather than writing the code.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.570
So what do we do then?

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:13.520
So if I have to imagine how
software development should be,

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:17.430
I think there are three
things that I want to be true.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:20.630
The first thing is that I
want as a software engineer,

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:23.780
I want to write code
in any language I want.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.660
And I want that code to
run exactly the same way

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:27.777
everywhere.

00:11:27.777 --> 00:11:29.360
Because I don't want
to be constrained

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:30.930
by certain languages.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.900
And when I run my
code on my laptop,

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:34.730
I want to make sure
that it exactly

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:36.990
runs the same way in
the cloud as well.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:38.700
I want this to be true.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.820
The second thing is
that I want my code

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:44.150
to be optimally deployed
somewhere and managed

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:44.900
by someone.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.190
But I shouldn't really
worry about that myself,

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.040
because I'm solving problems.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.230
I'm thinking about
business problems.

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:53.615
I don't necessarily need to
figure out every single time

00:11:53.615 --> 00:11:55.490
where should I put this
code or is the server

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:56.630
the right place to put it.

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.040
Someone has to do that for me.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.720
I mean you would think that
at this point in software

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:01.261
development, we will
get to the point

00:12:01.261 --> 00:12:03.734
where this is done for us.

00:12:03.734 --> 00:12:05.400
And the third thing
is that I don't even

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:07.735
want to think about machines.

00:12:07.735 --> 00:12:10.110
I want to think about problems
and solving those problems

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:10.620
with code.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:13.650
I want machines to be available
to me when I need them.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.540
I want them to scale
up when they need to.

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.289
I want them to scale
down as they need to.

00:12:17.289 --> 00:12:19.220
If I need some
kind of data store,

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:20.705
I want that to be
available to me.

00:12:20.705 --> 00:12:22.080
I shouldn't worry
about machines,

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.163
provisioning machines, and
all that kind of stuff.

00:12:24.163 --> 00:12:25.300
So this is what I want.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:29.590
And if you look at the last
few years in the industry,

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.780
we are actually moving
in this direction.

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:35.730
So when we think about writing
your code in any language

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.250
and running it anywhere,
that's basically

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.840
what containers are all about.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.090
So Docker and Rocket,
they're basically

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:46.410
trying to create this context
for your applications,

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:49.500
where the context really
transfers from your laptop

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.610
to your cloud.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:53.100
And your application
runs in that context

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:54.810
in a consistent way.

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:57.300
When we think about
your app being optimally

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.490
deployed and managed,
that's the whole point

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.930
of container
management platforms

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.000
like Kubernetes and
Mesos and Docker Swarm

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.060
and things like that.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.660
So all they are trying to
do is run your containers

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.919
in a consistent way anywhere.

00:13:08.919 --> 00:13:10.710
And the third thing
about all the resources

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:12.540
being available when
you need them, well,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.130
that's what the
cloud is all about.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:16.820
So Google Cloud and
other cloud providers,

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.220
they're all trying to make it
so that the resources that you

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:22.387
need is available
when you need them.

00:13:22.387 --> 00:13:23.970
So we are going in
the right direction

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:27.360
and we have tools and
clouds to do the job.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.180
So we'll take a look at
Kubernetes today, and see

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.990
how that provides what we need.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.280
So now what I'm
going to do is, first

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.522
I want to build a really
simple Hello World service.

00:13:41.522 --> 00:13:43.230
And I'm going to do
that in Google Cloud.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:44.855
And then later we'll
take this service,

00:13:44.855 --> 00:13:46.710
and we'll containerize
it using Docker,

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.060
and see how that looks like.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.820
And once it's containerized,
we'll take the container image,

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.970
push it to Google Cloud so that
we can use it in other places,

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:56.310
and then we're going to
create a Kubernetes cluster.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:58.590
And we'll use that container
in Kubernetes cluster.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:02.170
So through that we'll learn
what Kubernetes is all about.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.530
So first let's, look
at our microservice.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.770
And for this I'm using an
ASP .NET Core application.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:11.090
So are there any
.NET developers here?

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:11.590
No?

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:12.280
OK.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:15.460
Well .NET used to run on
Windows and nowhere else.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:20.320
But recently ASP .NET Core
came along and it is basically

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:23.480
a multiplatform version of .NET
that runs on Windows, Linux,

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:24.730
and Mac.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:27.950
And I'm really impressed by the
fact that .NET runs on Linux.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:29.310
So I'm using that in my demos.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:31.780
So we'll use that application
as a starting point

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:33.020
for our microservice.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:35.650
But you can use any kind
of application here.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:39.040
The language doesn't
really matter.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.380
So here I am Google
Cloud Console.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.884
This is the main console
to manage our projects

00:14:43.884 --> 00:14:44.550
in Google Cloud.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.980
I activate Cloud Shell.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:48.230
This gives you a development
environment in the cloud.

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.310
So by clicking there, I'm
basically getting a Linux box

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:52.269
somewhere in the cloud.

00:14:52.269 --> 00:14:53.643
And I also have
a home page where

00:14:53.643 --> 00:14:54.880
I can create my applications.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:58.820
So I am in browser, and I
can access to my Linux box.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:00.850
So what I'm going to do
is I'm going to first--

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:04.330
so if I just type dotnet, that's
the command line tool to create

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:05.207
.NET applications.

00:15:05.207 --> 00:15:06.790
It's already installed
in Cloud Shell.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:08.749
So I don't need to worry
about installing that.

00:15:08.749 --> 00:15:10.540
What I'm going to do
is I'm going to create

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:11.920
a folder for my application.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:15.280
I'm going to call it
Hello World ASP .NET Core.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:16.690
And then once I
have that folder,

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:19.480
I'm going to use the dotnet
command line tool to create

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.560
a Hello World application.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.880
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to say dotnet New-TWeb.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.989
So this creates that web
Hello World application.

00:15:27.989 --> 00:15:29.530
And if you look at
the files, there's

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.196
a lot of files they
have created for us.

00:15:31.196 --> 00:15:34.730
Program.js is the main entry
point to my application.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:36.460
So I'm going to edit that file.

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:40.480
And I'm going to add a single
line to the program of js,

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:42.957
to make sure that my
application binds to port 8080.

00:15:42.957 --> 00:15:45.040
Because I want to be able
to test this application

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:46.220
from the browser.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.760
So to do that, I want to make
sure it starts on port 8080.

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:54.820
So I'm adding one line that
says [INAUDIBLE] in HTTP *8080.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.770
This way, my application,
when it starts up,

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:59.710
it starts on port 8080.

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.000
So I go back.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.550
The first thing that I need
to do is dotnet restore.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:06.940
So this basically it looks
at the project .JSON file.

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:09.970
This is the file that
determines the dependencies

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:10.979
on my application.

00:16:10.979 --> 00:16:12.520
It will find the
dependencies, and it

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:13.900
will download them from NuGet.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:16.330
NuGet is the dependency
management platform on .NET.

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.920
So we'll go to NuGet,
restore the dependencies

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:20.950
so that I have all
the dependencies here.

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:23.540
Once I have that I
can do dotnet run.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.320
And this will run the
application on the Linux box.

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:28.940
And then we will preview and
makes sure that it works.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.270
And once it works, we
can containerized it.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.080
So I'm doing dotnet run.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.170
So this will compile the
application on Linux,

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:39.540
and then it will
run it on port 8080.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:42.884
So once we see the prompt that
it's running on port 8080,

00:16:42.884 --> 00:16:44.550
we know that the
application is working.

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:48.000
And there's a nice feature
of Cloud Shell, Web Preview,

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.960
so if I click that, it will find
the Linux box that I'm running.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:52.840
And it will bind to
port 8080 automatically.

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:55.480
So I don't have to worry about
the IPs and stuff like that.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:58.560
So now my app is
running on Linux.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:01.500
So nothing fancy, but we'll
use this as a reference app

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:02.500
to container SM1.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:09.520
So we've got our app.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:10.719
Next step is containers.

00:17:10.719 --> 00:17:12.760
I mean why should we even
bother with containers?

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:15.710
So let's try to answer
that question first.

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:18.849
If you look at what we used
to do up until recently,

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:20.950
we used to do
something like this.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:22.887
We would have a
physical machine.

00:17:22.887 --> 00:17:24.970
We would install the kernel,
the operating system,

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:26.044
on the machine.

00:17:26.044 --> 00:17:28.210
And then we will install
all the tools and libraries

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:30.190
that we need for
our applications.

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:33.426
And then we'll deploy our
applications on top of that.

00:17:33.426 --> 00:17:35.050
The good thing about
this is that there

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:37.450
was a single machine,
single operating system,

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:38.450
single set of libraries.

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:40.430
So it was easy.

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.730
But then the bad thing
is that the applications,

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:45.147
they don't have any isolation
whatsoever from each other.

00:17:45.147 --> 00:17:47.230
So if you change something
in the operating system

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:49.180
or if you change something
in the libraries,

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:51.860
you could potentially break
any of these applications.

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:55.210
So it was easy to do, but
very easy to break as well.

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.050
Then we had virtual machines.

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:58.990
The idea with virtual
machines is that you

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:00.910
have a single physical machine.

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:04.137
But then you virtualize
the operating system

00:18:04.137 --> 00:18:05.720
and the libraries
or your application.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.110
So you put them in
a virtual machine.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.289
The good thing here is that
applications are totally

00:18:10.289 --> 00:18:11.330
separate from each other.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:12.894
So they are totally isolated.

00:18:12.894 --> 00:18:14.560
So if you change
something in one place,

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:16.143
it's not going effect
the other place.

00:18:16.143 --> 00:18:18.430
But the bad thing is that
you have four operating

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.020
systems and four
set of libraries

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:21.290
that you need to worry about.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:23.330
So this is a lot of
management overhead.

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:25.300
So what has happened
is that people use

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.615
virtual machines in production.

00:18:26.615 --> 00:18:28.240
But they didn't really
use them as they

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:29.200
developed their applications.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:31.390
Because it was a pain to
do, to manage all the stuff

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:32.500
from your laptop.

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:35.530
So it was more like a
deployment time kind

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:37.090
of thing, the virtual machines.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:39.844
So with containers, you
have best of both worlds.

00:18:39.844 --> 00:18:42.010
So in containers, you have
a single physical machine

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:44.090
and a single operating system.

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:46.150
But you are virtualizing
from libraries

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:48.840
up, so your application
and its dependencies.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:51.319
You're virtualizing them in
what's called a container.

00:18:51.319 --> 00:18:53.110
And these containers,
they are much smaller

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:54.060
than virtual machines.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.900
So they're much
more lightweight.

00:18:55.900 --> 00:19:00.370
So this way it's simple because
it's one machine, one operating

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:01.309
system.

00:19:01.309 --> 00:19:03.100
But it's isolated as
well at the same time.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:05.570
So it's combining the
best of both worlds.

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:06.550
So what is a container?

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:08.320
As I mentioned, it's
a lightweight way

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:11.180
of virtualizing
your applications.

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:12.562
They are sealed and isolated.

00:19:12.562 --> 00:19:14.020
So you can have
multiple containers

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:15.460
on a single operating
system, and they don't

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:16.850
interfere with each other.

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:18.830
They are easy to deploy.

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.380
So compared to
virtual machines, you

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:23.670
can deploy stuff to a
container in seconds.

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.540
They're easier to introspect.

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:28.210
So you can actually SSH into
the container, look at what's

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:30.940
running, you can look at the
logs; all that kind of stuff.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:33.190
And for me the most important
part is it's composable.

00:19:33.190 --> 00:19:34.340
They are easy to compose.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:37.480
So what you can have is
that there's a base image

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:39.260
that you depend on.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:41.620
And then you can build your
image and your application

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.390
in a separate image that
depends on the base image.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:46.856
And if people need to
depend on your application,

00:19:46.856 --> 00:19:48.730
they can build an image
on top of your image.

00:19:48.730 --> 00:19:51.055
So this way you can
layer your dependencies.

00:19:51.055 --> 00:19:52.180
And this is very important.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.700
Because that way if
someone depends on you,

00:19:54.700 --> 00:19:57.380
all they have to do is get your
image and build on top of that.

00:19:57.380 --> 00:19:58.660
So that's nice.

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:01.390
Oh, I should also mention,
Docker is the main container

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:02.830
platform that most people use.

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:05.380
But there are others,
like Rocket from CoreOS.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:07.781
And I'm sure there will
be more going forward.

00:20:07.781 --> 00:20:09.280
And also containers,
they might look

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:10.821
like there's something
new because we

00:20:10.821 --> 00:20:13.545
started talking about them in
the last two or three years.

00:20:13.545 --> 00:20:14.920
But at Google,
we've been running

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:17.740
containers for a long time,
for more than 12 years.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:18.960
Everything in Google--

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:22.520
Gmail, search, even the virtual
machines within Google Cloud

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.370
perform; they run
within containers.

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:26.740
Because we like the
security element

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.890
of containers and our
whole infrastructure

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:32.380
is optimized for containers.

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:35.151
And actually we launched more
than 2 billion containers

00:20:35.151 --> 00:20:35.650
per week.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.480
So you can see the scale
of containers at Google.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:40.330
So now let's take our
simple application,

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:42.770
and try to containerize it,
and see how that looks like.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:47.330
So here I am in
Cloud Shell again.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:48.464
I have my application.

00:20:48.464 --> 00:20:49.880
First, what I need
to do is I need

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:53.780
to put all my application
and its dependencies

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:55.190
into a single DLL.

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:57.344
So this is a single
package for my application

00:20:57.344 --> 00:20:58.760
basically that I'm
going to build.

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:01.940
To do that I run .NET
publish-c release.

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:03.544
So this will compile
my application.

00:21:03.544 --> 00:21:05.210
It will compile all
of its dependencies,

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:07.250
and it will create a single DLL.

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:09.680
Later I'm going to use
this DLL from Docker.

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:11.160
So Docker will call this DLL.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:13.440
So that's why I'm doing this.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.440
So as this is a
building, next step

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:17.530
will be to build what's
called a Docker file.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:21.290
A Docker file is basically
a blueprint for an image.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:26.480
So it basically tells Docker
how to build your image.

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:27.892
So we'll create a Docker file.

00:21:27.892 --> 00:21:29.600
And we'll take a look
at the Docker file.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:33.330
Because it's very important
to see what's in there.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:35.150
So we build our DLL.

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:38.150
And next we're going to copy
a Docker file that I created

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:39.530
into the release folder.

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.990
And we'll take a look at that.

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:46.280
So DLL is built. And let's
just go to the release folder

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:47.730
where my DLLs are.

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:50.510
I'll just double check
that everything is there.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.070
Yes, I have my DLLs.

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:54.440
I only need the one that
I just built, actually.

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:55.815
So what I'm going
to do right now

00:21:55.815 --> 00:21:58.771
is I'm copying the Docker file
that I already created to here.

00:21:58.771 --> 00:22:00.770
And now we're going to
take a look at that file.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:02.280
Because it's very important.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:06.110
So let's just pause
here for a second.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:09.980
So this Docker file, the
first line says from Microsoft

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:11.500
dotnet 101 runtime.

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:13.810
So this is the image
that I depend on.

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:15.970
So this is ASP .NET
Core application.

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:19.300
And it depends on an image
provided by Microsoft

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:21.820
called dotnet 101 runtime.

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:23.800
So this is the runtime
image that I depend on.

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:26.050
The second line says copy.app.

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.810
So it says copy everything
in this folder, the DLLs

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:32.170
to the app folder
in the container.

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:35.050
Then the third line says
the working directory app,

00:22:35.050 --> 00:22:37.420
so the app folder
in the container

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:38.950
will be the working directory.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:41.130
And then expose 8080.

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:43.540
So this will expose the
port 8080 in the container.

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:46.310
And the other one is an
environment variable.

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:48.730
So this is ASP .NET Core URLs.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:50.860
So I am basically setting
an environment variable

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:53.650
to make sure that my
application starts on port 8080.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:55.630
And the last line
is entry point.

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:57.490
So what happens when
the container starts?

00:22:57.490 --> 00:22:59.260
So here I'm calling dotnet.

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:02.020
This is the command line tool
to run .NET applications.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:04.480
And then I'm pointing to
the DLL that I just built.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:08.455
So if you think about it, the
first line is my dependency.

00:23:08.455 --> 00:23:12.370
The second through the
last line is my environment

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:13.246
that I'm setting.

00:23:13.246 --> 00:23:14.620
And the last line
is what happens

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:15.840
when the container starts.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:18.550
So with one file, I'm basically
defining my dependencies,

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:21.400
my environment, and what happens
when the container starts.

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:23.657
This is great, because
without this, you

00:23:23.657 --> 00:23:26.240
have to basically tell people,
install this, install that, set

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:27.930
this environment this way.

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:29.680
So you have to give
all these instructions

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:31.540
and make sure that they
follow it properly.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:32.890
But with Docker, you
don't have to do that.

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:35.050
You can just specify what
you want it to be true,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:36.110
and it makes it happen.

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:37.740
So that's great.

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:38.865
So this is the Docker file.

00:23:38.865 --> 00:23:40.281
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:23:40.281 --> 00:23:42.690
going to now build an image
from this Docker file.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:46.450
So the image will basically
look at this Docker file.

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:49.090
And it will build an
image that we can use.

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:51.270
So to do that, we use
docker command line tool.

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:52.853
It's already installed
in Cloud Shell.

00:23:52.853 --> 00:23:54.600
So you don't have to
install it yourself.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:58.360
So I'm saying Docker
build-t means tag my image.

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:00.610
So I'm tagging it
with some data.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:04.140
So gcr.io always refers for
Google Container Repository.

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:06.830
Because I'm going to push this
image through Google Cloud

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:08.560
Container Repository later.

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:10.590
And then Hello.net is
the name on my image,

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:12.670
and V1 is the
version on my image.

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:16.620
So it's based on looking
at the Docker file.

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:18.460
And it's going
through the steps.

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:21.600
The first step is my
dependency, what I depend on.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.450
And it's an image
from Microsoft.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:25.300
I don't have it.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:27.390
So we are downloading
that from Docker Hub Base.

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:30.020
So it's getting the
image from Docker Hub,

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:33.280
and it will save it
to local Linux box.

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:34.530
So we're getting the image.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.905
And then once we
have that image,

00:24:35.905 --> 00:24:38.180
we'll go through
the other steps.

00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:39.900
We'll copy files,
and we'll set up

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:42.429
our port, and all
that kind of stuff.

00:24:42.429 --> 00:24:43.470
So we'll let that happen.

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:54.150
So the image is built. So if
we actually do Docker images,

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:58.090
we should see both our image
and our dependency image.

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:00.849
So if you do Docker images,
the first one is my image,

00:25:00.849 --> 00:25:02.890
and the second one is the
image that I depend on.

00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:04.416
So they're both on this machine.

00:25:04.416 --> 00:25:06.790
Now I want to run this image
and make sure that it works.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:11.730
So to do that I'll Docker run-d
it means run in the background.

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:16.350
And then -p means port, so I'm
mapping port 8080 on my machine

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:18.350
to port 8080 in the container.

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.850
And I'm just pointing to
my image that I just built.

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:25.110
So this basically runs
the image locally.

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:28.156
And once this is done,
I get a code hash code

00:25:28.156 --> 00:25:29.280
that tells me it's running.

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:31.650
If I do Docker PS, I
see that it is running.

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:33.390
And then if I do web
preview, I should

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.730
see the same application.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.070
But the difference in this
case is that this application

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:42.634
is running within Docker, within
the Docker Container basically.

00:25:42.634 --> 00:25:43.300
So there you go.

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:44.190
The image is running.

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:45.210
So the next thing
that we're going to do

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.100
is once I have the
image, I'm going

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:49.380
to push that image
to Google Cloud.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:53.440
So I'll do Docker stop
to stop my application.

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:56.359
And then I'll do a
GCloud Docker push.

00:25:56.359 --> 00:25:58.650
So this way, I'm basically
taking the image that I just

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.275
built locally, and
I'm going to push it

00:26:00.275 --> 00:26:01.822
to Google Container Repository.

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:06.959
So we'll fast forward this.

00:26:06.959 --> 00:26:08.500
Because we don't
have that much time.

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:10.030
But then what you will
see is that basically

00:26:10.030 --> 00:26:11.904
at the end of this, the
image will be pushed.

00:26:11.904 --> 00:26:14.840
And we will be able to use
this image from Google Cloud.

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:16.090
So let's just get out of here.

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:23.217
So containers are
good, because now I

00:26:23.217 --> 00:26:25.050
have an image that I
can push it to anywhere

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:26.260
and use it from anywhere.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:27.870
So it gives me the
context I need.

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:30.600
But then to run
this in production,

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:32.740
there are still questions
that you need to answer.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:36.120
For example, if you want
to have redundant services,

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:38.460
you need to run multiple
copies of these containers.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:39.960
So you need to do that yourself.

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:42.251
If you want resiliency, you
need to create an endpoint.

00:26:42.251 --> 00:26:43.626
If you want to
scale up and down,

00:26:43.626 --> 00:26:44.834
you need to do that yourself.

00:26:44.834 --> 00:26:47.040
So all this stuff that you
need to do in production,

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:48.390
you still need to do that.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:51.220
Docker or containers, they
don't really help for that.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:53.910
So that's when
Kubernetes comes in.

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:56.130
So Kubernetes,
it's a Greek word.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.461
And it stands for governor.

00:26:58.461 --> 00:26:59.960
Actually you're
right they of saying

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:02.900
Kubernetes is Kuvernetes.

00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:04.560
But I guess it
started in the US,

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:06.400
and everyone calls it wrong.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:08.610
But if you don't learn
anything in this talk,

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.310
just learn that it's
called Kuvernetes.

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:14.760
Basically it's a container
management platform.

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.220
It's 100% open source,
written and go.

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:19.690
It's supported by
companies like Google, IBM,

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:21.450
and many other big companies.

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:23.730
The greatest thing
about Kubernetes for me

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:25.860
is that it runs on
multiple cloud providers.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:28.140
So you can have a
Kubernetes cluster that

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:31.470
runs on AWS, that can
run on Google Cloud,

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.900
and you can even have a
single Kubernetes cluster that

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:35.472
spans multiple cloud providers.

00:27:35.472 --> 00:27:36.930
So that's the great
thing about it.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:39.480
It came from
something called Borg,

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:41.319
which is an internal
container management

00:27:41.319 --> 00:27:42.610
platform that we use at Google.

00:27:42.610 --> 00:27:44.465
So people who worked
on Borg, they basically

00:27:44.465 --> 00:27:45.840
learned from that
over the years.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:48.580
And they created Kubernetes
for everyone else.

00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:51.280
So at the high level,
this is how it looks like.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:55.530
So you have a master that
has multiple things like API

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.380
servers and SCTs, and
schedulers and controllers.

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:00.660
And then you have nodes
where you actually

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:01.857
schedule containers.

00:28:01.857 --> 00:28:03.690
They talk through
something called kubelets.

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:06.630
And then as a user,
you talk to the master,

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:09.970
either through APIs, CLI, or UI.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:12.040
So at the high
level, if you want

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:13.750
to use Kubernetes
in Google Cloud,

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:17.740
you will basically build an
image through a Docker file.

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:19.870
You push that image to
Google Container Registry.

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:20.954
We just did that.

00:28:20.954 --> 00:28:22.620
And then from there,
then on, first, you

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:24.510
need to create a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:28.060
So you use G Cloud to
create a Kubernetes cluster.

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:30.520
And then from then
on, you use kubectl,

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:32.830
which is the command line
tool to manage Kubernetes,

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:37.591
to schedule your images
to a Kubernetes cluster.

00:28:37.591 --> 00:28:39.840
So now let's take a look at
how to create a Kubernetes

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:40.850
cluster in Google Cloud.

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:46.730
So first, as you
see, I push my image.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:51.190
And if I go to Google Console
and go to Container Registry,

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:53.710
I should see my image
already in Google Cloud.

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:54.510
So I go here.

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:55.480
There's Hello.net.

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:56.980
And then there's
different versions.

00:28:56.980 --> 00:28:58.870
But the version 1 is
the one that I pushed.

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:00.490
So now my image
lives in the cloud,

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:03.110
and I can use it in
many different places.

00:29:03.110 --> 00:29:05.045
One of those is Kubernetes.

00:29:05.045 --> 00:29:07.420
So what I'm going to do is
I'm going to use G Cloud again

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:08.336
to create the cluster.

00:29:08.336 --> 00:29:13.070
So I'll say G Cloud
container clusters create.

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:15.320
And this will create a
Kubernetes cluster for me.

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:16.160
I'll give it a name.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:18.660
So I'll call it
hello.net cluster.

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:20.640
And then I'm going to
specify how many nodes I

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:22.610
want to schedule my containers.

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:24.240
The number of nodes is 1.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:26.780
And then machine
types is standard.

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:28.281
So there are different
machine types

00:29:28.281 --> 00:29:29.655
in Google Cloud
that you can use.

00:29:29.655 --> 00:29:31.410
But I'm using standard
machine types here.

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:32.785
And then I also
want to make sure

00:29:32.785 --> 00:29:35.370
that this cluster
lives in Europe West.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:37.290
So for zone I'm
saying Europe West.

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:40.200
So with this one line
of G Cloud command,

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:42.410
I will get a cluster,
with a master,

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:45.000
and with a single node, where
I can schedule containers.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.860
And I can use that to
schedule my containers.

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.280
So if you go to
Container Engine,

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:53.080
I should see at some
point a cluster comes up.

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:55.410
So let's just fast-forward.

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:57.420
And at some point, I'll
see a cluster coming up.

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:59.294
And then from then on
I can use that cluster.

00:29:59.294 --> 00:30:01.657
So it's just one
command basically.

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:06.290
In the last 10
minutes, I'm going

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:08.060
to cover what Kubernetes is.

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:11.430
So Kubernetes, it comes with
a lot of building blocks.

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:12.650
So this is some of them.

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:13.839
And there's even more.

00:30:13.839 --> 00:30:15.380
We won't have time
to cover them all.

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:17.270
But feel free to stop
by, so I can tell

00:30:17.270 --> 00:30:19.010
you tell you more about them.

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:21.380
But at the high level, this
is how a Kubernetes cluster

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:22.280
looks like.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:23.709
You have your container.

00:30:23.709 --> 00:30:25.250
From your container
you create what's

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:28.760
called a pod, which is
either a single container

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:30.230
or multiple
containers, depending

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:31.430
on what you want to do.

00:30:31.430 --> 00:30:32.971
Then after that,
you create something

00:30:32.971 --> 00:30:34.416
called replication controller.

00:30:34.416 --> 00:30:36.290
So in a replication
controller, you basically

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:38.480
tell Kubernetes what
you want to be true.

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:40.020
Here I'm saying replicas, 3.

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:42.580
So I want three
copies of this pod.

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:43.982
Then Kubernetes
will look at that

00:30:43.982 --> 00:30:45.690
and say, OK, I need
to create three pods.

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:47.523
It creates three pods,
and it schedules them

00:30:47.523 --> 00:30:48.632
onto on some nodes.

00:30:48.632 --> 00:30:50.090
But that's something
you don't have

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:51.980
to care about, like
Kubernetes figures out

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:53.720
where to schedule these nodes.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:55.430
And then once these
pods are running,

00:30:55.430 --> 00:30:57.470
you can expose them
to the outside world

00:30:57.470 --> 00:30:59.030
using what's called a service.

00:30:59.030 --> 00:31:03.760
So service is kind of like a
load balancer for your pods.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:06.660
So, the first thing that I want
to talk about is deployment.

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:08.220
So in Kubernetes,
when you start you

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.260
create what's
called a deployment.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:13.410
It's basically a high-level
construct that you create.

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:17.490
And under the covers it creates
all the lower-level things

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:19.540
that's needed to
run your containers.

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:20.550
So it creates pods.

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:21.570
It creates replica sets.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.000
And it creates services for you.

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:26.880
And we'll look at what
those are shortly.

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:29.455
So let's just take a look at
how to create a deployment.

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:34.360
So here I am.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:35.600
My cluster is ready.

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:39.790
And then I want to basically
schedule my first container

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:41.500
to Kubernetes.

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:43.540
First to do that, I
need to use kubectl.

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:46.380
But I need to authenticate this
shell with Kubernetes cluster.

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:48.800
So to do that, I
just run a command.

00:31:48.800 --> 00:31:50.710
So once I run this
command, my shell

00:31:50.710 --> 00:31:52.730
is authenticated
with Kubernetes.

00:31:52.730 --> 00:31:55.790
And now I can use kubectl to
manage my Kubernetes cluster.

00:31:55.790 --> 00:32:00.040
So I'll say kubectl run, and
I'll give my deployment a name.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:02.349
So this is called hello.net.

00:32:02.349 --> 00:32:03.890
And then I'll just
point to an image.

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:07.360
So I push this image from
my machine to Google Cloud.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:09.070
So now since it's
in Google Cloud,

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:11.710
I can easily refer to
that and use the image.

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:12.810
And that's it.

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:13.990
So I just refer to my image.

00:32:13.990 --> 00:32:15.364
And then I also
want to make sure

00:32:15.364 --> 00:32:17.080
that the port 8080 is enabled.

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:19.900
So I'll say port 8080.

00:32:19.900 --> 00:32:22.487
And then with this, we
basically created a deployment

00:32:22.487 --> 00:32:23.070
in Kubernetes.

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:24.740
So it's just one command line.

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:26.680
So if you do kubectl
get deployment,

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:28.780
I should see that my
deployment is ready.

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:30.020
It's very fast.

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:32.140
And if I do kubectl
get pods, you

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.140
will see that I have
some pods being created.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:37.280
So it's says status,
continue creating.

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:39.940
So it's creating the container
and it's trying to schedule it.

00:32:39.940 --> 00:32:43.420
If I do get pods again, I
should see the status running.

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:45.280
So within a few
seconds, the container

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:46.420
is created and scheduled.

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:47.362
So that's great.

00:32:47.362 --> 00:32:48.820
And then there's
also a replica set

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:51.650
that's been created
that we'll talk about.

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:54.005
And then there's also a
service that's been created.

00:32:54.005 --> 00:32:55.630
And if you look at
the service, there's

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.734
an internal IP, cluster IP.

00:32:57.734 --> 00:32:58.900
But there is no external IP.

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:01.430
Because I haven't exposed this
pod to the outside world yet.

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:02.440
We'll do that next.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:05.350
So as you see, all the stuff
that I need is created.

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:09.490
So let's just briefly look at
what the rest of this stuff is.

00:33:09.490 --> 00:33:11.920
So pods, volumes, and
labels; so I'll just

00:33:11.920 --> 00:33:13.150
talk about them briefly.

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:16.060
So a pod is basically the
smallest scheduling unit

00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:17.230
in Kubernetes.

00:33:17.230 --> 00:33:20.510
Usually you have a single
container in a pod.

00:33:20.510 --> 00:33:22.360
But sometimes there are
cases where you have

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:24.250
multiple containers in a pod.

00:33:24.250 --> 00:33:26.470
For example, in this
case, I have a container

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:29.680
called file puller that pulls
some files from some content

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:30.580
manager.

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:32.374
And then it saves
it to a volume.

00:33:32.374 --> 00:33:33.790
And then there's
another container

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:36.160
called Web Server that
serves from the volume

00:33:36.160 --> 00:33:37.340
to the consumers.

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:40.680
So I want these two
containers and their storage--

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:43.790
that's called volume--
to live in a single unit.

00:33:43.790 --> 00:33:44.850
So that's what a pod is.

00:33:44.850 --> 00:33:46.849
A pod basically just
puts them all together.

00:33:46.849 --> 00:33:48.640
And Kubernetes will
schedule that together,

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:51.990
and it will kill them
together, basically.

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:53.470
And a volume, as
I mentioned, it's

00:33:53.470 --> 00:33:55.330
basic storage for the pods.

00:33:55.330 --> 00:33:57.580
And you can attach a volume
to many different things,

00:33:57.580 --> 00:34:00.890
like a persistent disk
or something like that.

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:03.760
So once you have your
pods, you can label them.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:08.679
Labeling is the way you group
things together in Kubernetes.

00:34:08.679 --> 00:34:10.570
So once you attach
labels to your pods,

00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:12.760
you can use selectors
to get your pods.

00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:14.449
So you can say, give
me all the pods,

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:16.331
all my application
using this way.

00:34:16.331 --> 00:34:18.580
Or if you want to get all
your pods off the front end,

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:22.030
you can say this way,
and stuff like that.

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:23.530
So once you have
your pods, then you

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:25.540
can use something
called replica set.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:29.870
Replicate set is the
favorite part of Kubernetes.

00:34:29.870 --> 00:34:33.400
So you can think of replicate
set as a big brother watching

00:34:33.400 --> 00:34:34.330
your pods.

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:37.330
So you tell replica set
what you want to be true,

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:38.830
and it makes sure
that what you want

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:40.810
is maintained at all times.

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:44.590
For example in this case, the
number of replicas is three.

00:34:44.590 --> 00:34:47.290
And then there's some selectors
I'm using. op equals demo,

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:49.580
and color is blue or gray.

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:52.300
So I'm telling replica set,
make sure you have three pods

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:55.239
at all times with these labels.

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:58.000
And then it will maintain
those pods on some nodes

00:34:58.000 --> 00:34:59.240
that I don't care about.

00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:01.060
And if any of these nodes--

00:35:01.060 --> 00:35:02.860
pods go down, it
will create a new pod

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:04.068
and schedule it on the nodes.

00:35:04.068 --> 00:35:07.540
So it will make sure
that this is always true.

00:35:07.540 --> 00:35:09.600
So I have a replica set.

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:11.370
Let me just show
you this quickly.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.740
And this is probably
going to be the last demo.

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:16.240
So here I have my pods running.

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:19.131
So what I'm going to do is
I'm going to delete this pod

00:35:19.131 --> 00:35:20.005
and see what happens.

00:35:20.005 --> 00:35:24.230
So I'm going to
say kubectl delete,

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:25.700
and then passing the pod ID.

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:27.680
So what happens right
now is replica set

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:28.800
is watching on my pod.

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:31.372
So it will detect right away
that the pod is deleted,

00:35:31.372 --> 00:35:32.580
and it will create a new one.

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:35.610
So if I do the delete, then
if I do get pods again,

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:38.800
you'll see a new pod with a new
ID has already been created.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:40.915
It's says status,
continue creating.

00:35:40.915 --> 00:35:43.040
So replica set, watches
basically your pod.

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:45.334
And as soon as it's
deleted or something,

00:35:45.334 --> 00:35:47.000
it will create a new
one and schedule it

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:48.496
within seconds, which is good.

00:35:48.496 --> 00:35:50.120
Because I don't have
to do that myself.

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:55.809
And then so you have your
pods, you have your labels,

00:35:55.809 --> 00:35:58.100
and then you have your replica
sets watching your pods.

00:35:58.100 --> 00:36:01.260
Now you're ready to expose
your pods to the outside world.

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:04.160
So you'll do that through
what's called services.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:07.100
So the idea of service
is that you basically

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:11.090
use a selector to select the
pods that you want to expose.

00:36:11.090 --> 00:36:12.920
In this case, I am
selecting all the parts

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:15.020
that have type equals FE.

00:36:15.020 --> 00:36:16.820
So all the pods
that have that label

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:18.680
will be exposed
behind the service,

00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:20.630
and the service will
have a stable virtual IP.

00:36:20.630 --> 00:36:23.150
And the client will talk to
the service to get to the pods.

00:36:23.150 --> 00:36:27.270
So to actually expose
our pod, it's again,

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:30.780
one line of code, command.

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:32.390
So I have my deployment running.

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:34.810
I have my pods running.

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:36.680
And if I want to
expose this pod,

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:40.340
all I have to say is
kubectl expose deployment,

00:36:40.340 --> 00:36:42.980
pass in my deployment
ID, which is hello.net.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:45.080
And then I will say type
equals load balancer.

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:48.130
So this will basically create a
load balancing in Google Cloud.

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:52.310
It will attach that to
our Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:54.561
And once the load
balancer has an IP,

00:36:54.561 --> 00:36:56.810
we can hit that IP and it
will go through the service,

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:57.934
and it will get to the pod.

00:36:57.934 --> 00:37:00.630
So here if I do
kubectl get service,

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:03.110
we'll see that the
external IP is pending.

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:07.160
Because it's creating a
load balancer right now.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:10.980
And I will fast-forward because
we don't have that much time.

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:13.050
At some point when I
do kubectl get service,

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:17.841
I will have an external IP,
maybe on here, further down.

00:37:17.841 --> 00:37:19.670
Yeah, now I have
the external IP.

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:21.787
So what this means is
that if I hit that IP,

00:37:21.787 --> 00:37:23.370
I will hit the load
balancer, and that

00:37:23.370 --> 00:37:25.150
will route me to the right pod.

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:26.940
So this is the
same code running,

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:29.799
but it's running within
a Docker container.

00:37:29.799 --> 00:37:31.340
And it's being
managed by Kubernetes.

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:35.130
So Kubernetes watches
that container for us.

00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:36.820
I only have two minutes.

00:37:36.820 --> 00:37:38.460
So that means I
have to speak really

00:37:38.460 --> 00:37:40.330
fast in the last two minutes.

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:43.950
But what I want to tell you
is so scaling in Kubernetes

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:45.280
becomes really easy.

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:48.000
So if you have three pods,
to scale to four pods,

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:51.037
all you have to do is just tell
replica set you want four pods,

00:37:51.037 --> 00:37:52.620
and it would just
do it automatically,

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:54.729
with just one line
of code, very easy.

00:37:54.729 --> 00:37:55.770
You can set auto scaling.

00:37:55.770 --> 00:37:57.420
So you have your pods.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:59.220
You can deploy something
called Heapster,

00:37:59.220 --> 00:38:02.640
which is a real cool name,
that will collect all the CPU

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:04.290
metrics, if that's
what you want.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:06.000
And it will report
to replicate set.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.460
And replica set will
look at its rules.

00:38:08.460 --> 00:38:12.010
And it will determine if the
CPU target is more than 50%,

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:15.000
create a new pod, and
it will just scale.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:17.390
And I have a demo,
but I have to skip.

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:19.344
Then you can do
canary deployments.

00:38:19.344 --> 00:38:20.760
So you can have
two replicate sets

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:22.450
with two different versions.

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:25.470
But you can expose them
behind the same service

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:27.190
and do canary deployments.

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:28.810
And then you can
the rolling updates.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.200
So let's say you have a
version 1 of your application,

00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:34.410
and you want to do an
update to the version 2,

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:37.110
you create a new replica
set in version 2,

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:39.180
and then add the
new pod to that.

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:43.080
And you expose everything
behind the same service.

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:45.450
And if everything looks
good, you take one out here,

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:48.030
you add one here, you take one
out there, you add one here,

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:50.170
you take one out there;
and if everything works,

00:38:50.170 --> 00:38:51.461
you get rid of the replica set.

00:38:51.461 --> 00:38:54.540
So to do this in Kubernetes,
it's again one command.

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:57.210
You just say update,
do a rolling update,

00:38:57.210 --> 00:38:59.680
and it does all of this
for you automatically.

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:01.642
So I have a demo.

00:39:01.642 --> 00:39:02.850
But I can't show that to you.

00:39:02.850 --> 00:39:04.010
Because we don't have time.

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:06.912
But feel free to stop
by after the talk.

00:39:06.912 --> 00:39:08.120
And then there's other stuff.

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:09.670
There's DaemonSets,
there are jobs,

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:11.200
there's config maps and secrets.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:12.610
But we won't cover them here.

00:39:12.610 --> 00:39:14.540
Just know that they exist.

00:39:14.540 --> 00:39:17.380
So there's lots of
constructs basically

00:39:17.380 --> 00:39:21.130
that makes your life easier
when you want to run containers

00:39:21.130 --> 00:39:22.870
in production.

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:23.830
There's even more.

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:25.090
I didn't put it here.

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:27.580
But if you want to learn
more, kubernetes.io

00:39:27.580 --> 00:39:28.437
is the page to go.

00:39:28.437 --> 00:39:30.270
If you want to learn
about Container Engine,

00:39:30.270 --> 00:39:32.530
cloud.google.com
/containerengine.

00:39:32.530 --> 00:39:34.870
And if you want to get the
slides, follow me on Twitter.

00:39:34.870 --> 00:39:36.286
And if you gave
any questions, I'm

00:39:36.286 --> 00:39:38.290
going to be outside
the experts area.

00:39:38.290 --> 00:39:41.040
Thanks a lot for listening.

